it's been a devastating year in  California with wildfires burning across  the state they're blamed for the deaths  of at least eight people and the loss of  countless homes so far more than 4,000  fires have burned nearly eight hundred  eighteen thousand acres this summer's  Mendocino complex fire is responsible  for more than half of that destruction  the river and ranch fires combined make  up the biggest complex fire in the  history of California the flames are  leaving a trail of devastation but out  of the ashes the seed of an idea was  planted for a local artist Jamie wax is  here with his story Jamie good morning  good morning Elaine Darren's Sarkin is  making art to shine a light on the  ongoing crisis in California two things  he knows a lot about working in his Los  Angeles studio Darren's Arkin designs  lighting and installations for movie  studios musicians and corporate events I  try to take inspiration from what I find  in the world and usually that is more  meaningful than anything that I want to  create this latest project is personal  it began just over two years ago after a  wildfire tore through the Angeles  National Forest the sand fire as it was  called would torch more than 40,000  acres of land in less than two weeks  what's Arkin found driving through the  area was shocking and suddenly around me  the hillsides were covered in black ash  and tree nubs sticking out of the soil  so I got out and something encouraged me  to drag few pieces of wood back to my  car and then I just had them in my house  wondering what am I gonna do with this  the answer bring wood back to life he  meticulously carves grooves through the  wood which he then fills with strips of  LED lights  he adds shards of recycled glass and  applies polyurethane to preserve the  finished product when you're working  with these pieces what are you chasing  after what are you trying to make  I'm trying to find some beauty in the  ugliness trying to bring life to death  his pieces are now part of an  installation titled burning light which  is currently on display at the West  Hollywood gallery radiant space I had  gathered a couple small pieces of wood  before I came across this the charcoal  falls apart so easily yeah that I was  trying to delicately haul 100-pound log  back to my car and keep it from breaking  too much Sarkin has always been drawn to  the outdoors when he's not working he's  hiking and scaling mountains that  passion for nature has fueled the  burning light project the initial  inspiration for this came when I was  climbing in El Capitan in Yosemite in  2015 on the ground all around us  beautiful green lush nature but at about  a thousand feet up suddenly seeing  everything from above could just see how  dry and dying the forest looked this is  you trying to fix that in some small way  yes another influence for Sarkin is his  cousin a firefighter for the Ventura  County Fire Department currently there  are 8,000 firefighters on the frontlines  fighting six significant fires across  the state now here we are two and a half  years later in the midst of this  installation and California is again  ravaged by wildfires is that a visceral  experience for you right now sitting  inside of this installation absolutely  feel what inspires me most about this  kind of work is that it brings attention  to what's going on around us the the  timeliness of this is total accident  it's painful over the past five years  fires have engulfed nearly every corner  of California satellite photos capture  the plumes of smoke across the state and  according to NOAA  the smoke has reached all the way to the  East Coast  making burning  light a uniquely modern work of art it's  great when art meets a moment like this  right yes it's hard for me to say great  because what is happening is is awful  but great that people can have a more  clear understanding and hopefully we can  come together around that working with  his cousin Sarkin selected the wildfire  related charity kal fund to receive ten  percent from all the pieces sold in the  current installation so deeply personal  jamie i loved when you asked him what  are you chasing after and he said i'm  trying to find some beauty in the  ugliness trying to bring life to death  it's the truth you know there's  something about the the glow and the  space that's been put into these burnt  pieces of wood that seems to have  exposed the heart of the pieces the  heart of the wood it looks as though you  have to see it in person to really get  it definitely oh wow
 

